// Module-level relationships and the overview of all eleven modules (ten library areas plus the host). The component-level edges
// live in each area file; the module-level edges below summarise them for the `overview` view.
//
// Sources (module dependencies were taken from #include evidence, then checked against the calls
// that the component edges record; a scan of every .c/.h in include/, src/, host/ and capsules/):
//   CMakeLists.txt:71-98     sig (core): base, value, capsule, service, persistence, project, runtime, loop, platform/posix
//   CMakeLists.txt:113-190   sig::mgmt (SIG_MGMT), sig::net (SIG_NET), sig::http_server (SIG_HTTP_SERVER, links sig_net), sig::signal_feed
//                            (SIG_SIGNAL_FEED, links sig_http_server), sig::http_client (SIG_HTTP_CLIENT), sig::mgmt_http (links sig_mgmt, sig_http_server)
//   CMakeLists.txt:20-40     SIG_SIGNAL_FEED needs SIG_HTTP_SERVER; SIG_HTTP_SERVER needs SIG_NET
//   host/CMakeLists.txt, host/posix/CMakeLists.txt   sig::host; sighost links sig_host, sig_builtin_capsules, and the optional modules
//   include/sig/runtime/sig_runtime.h        includes sig_capsule.h, sig_loop.h, sig_tap.h, sig_persistence.h, sig_project.h
//   include/sig/project/sig_project.h, sig_config.h   include sig_capsule.h, sig_persistence.h, sig_service.h
//   include/sig/runtime/sig_service.h        includes sig_capsule.h and sig_tap.h (the tap type; sig_tap.h lives in include/sig/runtime/)
//   src/runtime/sig_capsule.c:4              includes sig_service.h (enqueue, tap emit, schedule)
//   src/base/sig_log.c:2                     includes sig_thread.h (the log mutex)
//   src/platform/posix/sig_poller_*.c, src/net/sig_net_posix.c   include sig_alloc.h (and sig_log.h in sig_net_posix.c)
//   src/http/sig_http_server.c, include/sig/http/sig_http_server.h   include sig_runtime.h, sig_loop.h, sig_net.h, sig_thread.h, sig_time.h
//   src/feed/*.c, *.h                        include sig_http_server.h, sig_ws.h, sig_runtime.h, sig_tap.h, sig_loop.h, sig_service.h, sig_net.h
//   src/mgmt/*.c, include/sig/mgmt/*.h       include sig_runtime.h, sig_http_server.h (sig_mgmt_http.c), sig_thread.h, sig_time.h
//   host/sig_host.c                          includes only sig_host.h, sig_log.h and sig_loop.h (sig_host.h brings in the runtime and persistence types)
//   host/posix/main.c:10-25                  includes sig_persistence*.h, sig_capsule.h, sig_http_server.h, sig_mgmt*.h and sig_signal_feed.h (behind SIG_HOST_HAVE_*)
//   No file under include/ or src/ of the core (base, value, runtime, project, persistence, platform/posix) includes a header of net, http, mgmt or feed.
//   File-level include graph: no cycle (checked as strongly connected components over all headers and sources). The cycles below
//   appear only when files are grouped into the modules of this model.
//   capsules/weather/weather_capsule.h:5    includes <sig/http/sig_http_client.h> (angle brackets, missed by a scan for quoted includes); capsule.cmake links
//                                            sig::http_client; built only if weather is in SIG_CAPSULES and SIG_HTTP_CLIENT is on. The only such file under capsules/ or host/
//                                            apart from host/posix/main.c, which includes http, mgmt and feed headers behind SIG_HOST_HAVE_*.
//   How to read the edges below: they are hand-maintained summaries, not generated. Most have a component-level edge in an area file.
//   Edges backed by an include or call but with no component-level edge of their own:
//     runtime -> capsules     sig_runtime.h includes sig_capsule.h; sig_runtime_config.capsules is the borrowed registry
//     runtime -> persistence  sig_runtime.h includes sig_persistence.h; sig_runtime_config.store is borrowed and handed to sig_project_load
//     service -> runtime      sig_service.h:8 includes sig_tap.h (the tap type)
//     feed -> service         src/feed/sig_feed_hub.c includes sig_service.h and calls sig_service_name (line 264)
//   mgmt -> project, service, capsules are call-level: sig_mgmt.c reaches them through sig_runtime.h, it has no direct include of their headers.
//   Callback-style edges at component level (http -> mgmt glue, http -> feed upgrade, runtime/service -> feed tap) are left out on purpose: the
//   module edges point in the direction of the #include, and these run through a registered function pointer.
//   docs/architecture.md     "Layering": "Dependencies point downward only" is the claim this file tests (see the overview description)
model {
  sig.runtime -[calls]-> sig.project 'loads, owns and edits the project'
  sig.runtime -[calls]-> sig.service 'starts and stops services, sets driven mode and the tap'
  sig.runtime -[calls]-> sig.capsules 'borrows the capsule registry'
  sig.runtime -[calls]-> sig.persistence 'borrows the store'
  sig.runtime -[calls]-> sig.platform 'mutexes, conditions, clock, poller'
  sig.project -[calls]-> sig.persistence 'loads and saves the stored documents'
  sig.project -[calls]-> sig.capsules 'builds configurations and instances from the registry'
  sig.project -[calls]-> sig.service 'creates services and adds instances'
  sig.service -[calls]-> sig.capsules 'runs instance hooks and terminal handlers'
  sig.service -[calls]-> sig.platform 'worker thread, queue lock, clock'
  // (below) upward edges
  sig.service -[calls]-> sig.runtime 'upward: the tap type, from sig_tap.h' {
    style {
      color red
      line dashed
    }
  }
  sig.capsules -[calls]-> sig.service 'upward: enqueues signals, schedules jobs' {
    style {
      color red
      line dashed
    }
  }
  sig.foundation -[calls]-> sig.platform 'upward: the log mutex' {
    style {
      color red
      line dashed
    }
  }
  sig.capsules -[calls]-> sig.http 'upward, weather capsule only: sig_http_get_async (SIG_CAPSULES and SIG_HTTP_CLIENT)' {
    style {
      color red
      line dashed
    }
  }
  sig.http -[calls]-> sig.runtime 'runtime module; uses the loop of the runtime'
  sig.http -[calls]-> sig.platform 'sockets, threads, clock'
  sig.mgmt -[calls]-> sig.runtime 'service control, edits, shutdown'
  sig.mgmt -[calls]-> sig.project 'reads and edits through the runtime'
  sig.mgmt -[calls]-> sig.service 'service and instance state'
  sig.mgmt -[calls]-> sig.capsules 'registry, instances, routes'
  sig.mgmt -[calls]-> sig.http 'the glue registers the REST handler on the server'
  sig.mgmt -[calls]-> sig.platform 'reader/writer lock, clock'
  sig.feed -[calls]-> sig.http 'WebSocket upgrade on the server'
  sig.feed -[calls]-> sig.runtime 'runtime module; tap and terminal lookup'
  sig.feed -[calls]-> sig.service 'service name of a tapped signal'
  sig.feed -[calls]-> sig.platform 'threads, clock, sockets'
  sig.host -[calls]-> sig.runtime 'creates, starts and stops the runtime; registers modules'
  sig.host -[calls]-> sig.persistence 'opens the store'
  sig.host -[calls]-> sig.capsules 'creates the registry and fills it with the built-ins'
  sig.host -[calls]-> sig.http 'registers the server (with -m)'
  sig.host -[calls]-> sig.mgmt 'registers management and its glue (with -m)'
  sig.host -[calls]-> sig.feed 'registers the feed (with -m, when built)'
}

views {
  view overview {
    title 'Modules'
    description '
      The eleven modules (ten library areas plus the host) and how they depend on each other, with the CMake target
      of each. Edges into Foundation and from the other modules into Platform
      are left out (every module uses them); the one edge from Foundation to
      Platform is kept because it closes a cycle.
      "Dependencies point downward only" in docs/architecture.md holds for
      the optional layers with one exception: nothing in the core includes net,
      http, mgmt or feed, and the host is at the top, but the Capsules module
      reaches HTTP, because the weather capsule includes sig_http_client.h
      (it is built only when listed in SIG_CAPSULES and SIG_HTTP_CLIENT is
      on; no other capsule or shim file includes an optional header). That edge
      closes a further cycle, HTTP, Runtime, Capsules, HTTP. It does not hold
      inside the core. Edges here are summaries of the component
      edges; callbacks such as the HTTP server calling management or the
      feed are omitted.
      Four edges, drawn dashed in red, point upward: Foundation uses a mutex
      from Platform (the log), Capsules uses Service (to enqueue signals),
      Service uses a Runtime header (the tap type), and Capsules uses HTTP
      (weather). Together with the downward
      edges they make cycles between Runtime, Project, Service and Capsules,
      and between Foundation and Platform. No header cycle exists at file level.
      The function reference lives in generated pages: each component lists
      its functions in its metadata and links to the header pages that
      declare them; each module links to all of its header pages (including
      the type-only ones); the libsig system links to the index of all pages.
    '
    include sig.*
    exclude * -> sig.foundation
    exclude sig.runtime -> sig.platform
    exclude sig.service -> sig.platform
    exclude sig.http -> sig.platform
    exclude sig.mgmt -> sig.platform
    exclude sig.feed -> sig.platform
  }
}
